This is **{{HARNESS_NAME}}**'s entry point for `/tick`.

The rules are in `Tools/zagent/protocol/TICK.md` (repo root — a plain
backticked path, not a relative link, because this template is reused at a
different directory depth per harness: `.claude/commands/`, `.opencode/
commands/`, `.codex/skills/tick/`, ... and a single hardcoded `../` count
cannot be correct for all of them). That file
is harness-neutral: it names no harness, tool, or model, and speaks only in
its five control verbs (`DISPATCH`, `AWAIT`, `ABORT`, `SCHEDULE`, `STOP`),
its two roles (`WORKER`, `REVIEWER`) and its three tiers
(`haiku`/`sonnet`/`opus`). Everything below is how THIS harness resolves
those verbs, roles and tiers into concrete mechanism. When TICK.md and this
section disagree about what a verb or role MEANS on this harness, this
section wins — TICK.md does not know this harness exists.

{{TICK_BODY_OR_POINTER}}

## Controls

TICK.md's five control verbs, resolved to this harness's own mechanism.
`null` means no mechanism was found — read `{{HARNESS_ID}}`'s row in
`Tools/zagent/protocol/harnesses.json` for why, and treat that verb as
unavailable rather than guessing at a substitute.

| Verb | Resolves to |
| --- | --- |
| `DISPATCH` | {{CONTROL_DISPATCH}} |
| `AWAIT` | {{CONTROL_AWAIT}} |
| `ABORT` | {{CONTROL_ABORT}} |
| `SCHEDULE` | {{CONTROL_SCHEDULE}} |
| `STOP` | {{CONTROL_STOP}} |

`loopMode`: **{{LOOP_MODE}}**. When `manual`, TICK.md's own dispatch table
(step 1) already says what to do with each `next.json` disposition instead
of calling `SCHEDULE` — report and let a human re-invoke.

## Roles

Every routed worker tier and every reviewer risk maps 1:1 to a concrete
role file. There is no role file for a tier absent from this table —
`haiku` is deliberately unrouted (see `harnesses.json`'s `unusedTiers`) and
generates nothing.

| TICK.md role | Tier / risk | Role file |
| --- | --- | --- |
| WORKER | sonnet | {{ROLE_WORKER_SONNET}} |
| WORKER | opus | {{ROLE_WORKER_OPUS}} |
| REVIEWER | LOW | {{ROLE_REVIEWER_LOW}} |
| REVIEWER | MEDIUM | {{ROLE_REVIEWER_MEDIUM}} |
| REVIEWER | HIGH | {{ROLE_REVIEWER_HIGH}} |

**Worker tool denial:** `{{WORKER_TOOL_DENIAL}}`. When `enforced`, the role
file's own tool list IS invariant I1 — widen it and I1 is gone with nothing
else failing loudly, which is why role files sit under this harness's own
protected-path mechanism. When `declared`, the role's text is advisory
only and I1 is carried instead by the orchestrator's WORKER-MOVED-NOTHING
check at TICK.md step 6.4 — that check is not optional on this harness.

## Review policy

`review.mode`: **{{REVIEW_MODE}}**. `review.mode` only ever WIDENS what
TICK.md's own `review.required` (the OR of `reviewOn` and the
Definition-of-Done assertion scan) already asks for:

- `payload` — review exactly when `review.required` is true on the claim
  payload; no wider and no narrower.
- `always` — review every dispatch, regardless of the payload. There is no
  `never`: a harness profile can only add review, not remove it.

## Pre-authorisation

`{{PREAUTH_KIND}}`, sourced from: {{PREAUTH_SOURCES}}.

Every gate line `zagent gates <KEY>` can produce for this repo's categories
must be reachable through this mechanism before a tick runs unattended —
that is what "pre-authorised" means here, and `Tools/zagent/Test-HarnessParity.ps1`
checks it for all three harnesses so a gate line added without its
authorisation reds a gate instead of stalling an unattended loop on a
permission prompt.

